        "text": "from .. pkg3 import module4\n44     Using Modularization to Handle Complex Projects\nIn this case, we used two dots ( .. ) because module4.py is in the sibling folder of\nmodule1.py .\n\u2022 Scenario 3 : Importing Func2 inside module1.py :\nfrom .. pkg2.sub_pkg_1.module2 import Func2\nFor this scenario, we used two dots ( .. ) because the target module ( module2.py )\nis inside a folder that is in the sibling folder of module1.py . We used one dot to\naccess the sub_pkg_1 package and another dot to access module2 .\nOne advantage of relative imports is that they are simple and can significantly reduce\nlong import statements. But relative import statements can be messy and difficult to\nmaintain when projects are shared across teams and organizations. Relative imports are\nnot easy to read and manage.",
